WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.820
Bash norm processes your data one mini-batch at a time.

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:07.700
But at test time, you might need to process the examples one at a time.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:10.480
Let's see how you can adapt your network to do that.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:12.260
Recall that during training,

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:15.520
here are the equations you'd use to implement Bash norm.

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:16.980
Within a single mini-batch,

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:22.580
you'd sum over that mini-batch of the zi values to compute the mean.

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:26.020
So here, you're just summing over the examples in one mini-batch.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:29.300
I'm using m to denote the number of examples in the mini-batch,

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:31.500
not, not in the whole training set.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.380
Then you compute the variance and then you compute

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:38.600
z norm by scaling by the mean and standard deviation,

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.260
with epsilon added for numerical stability.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:46.940
And then z tilde is taking z norm every scaling by gamma and beta.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:51.440
So notice that mu and sigma squared,

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:54.600
which you need for this scaling calculation,

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.600
are computed on the entire mini-batch.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:01.400
But at test time, you might not have a mini-batch of 64,

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:05.280
128 or 256 examples to process at the same time.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.840
So you need some different way of coming up with mu and sigma squared.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:10.320
And if you have just one example,

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.560
taking the mean and variance of that one example, doesn't make sense.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:20.640
So what's actually done in order to apply your neural network at test time,

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:25.640
is to come up with some separate estimate of mu and sigma squared.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:28.240
And in typical implementations of batch norm,

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:35.240
what you do is estimate this using a exponentially weighted average,

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:40.800
where the average is across the mini-batches.

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:46.010
So to be very concrete, here's what I mean.

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:51.010
Let's pick some layer L and let's say you're going through mini-batches x1,

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:57.170
x2 together with the corresponding values of y and so on.

00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:02.330
So when training on x1, for that layer L,

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:05.690
you get some mu L.

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:10.690
And in fact, I'm going to write this as mu for the first mini-batch and that layer.

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:14.970
And then when you train on the second mini-batch,

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:16.890
for that layer and that mini-batch,

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:19.170
you end up with some second value of mu.

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:23.610
And then for the third mini-batch in this hidden layer,

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:28.610
you end up with some third value for mu.

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:34.250
So just as we saw how to use an exponentially weighted average

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:38.770
to compute the mean of theta 1, theta 2, theta 3,

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:42.570
when you are trying to compute a exponentially weighted average

00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:44.250
of the current temperature,

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:49.130
you would do that to keep track of what's the latest average value

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:51.090
of this mean vector you've seen.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:55.730
So that exponentially weighted average becomes your estimate

00:02:55.730 --> 00:02:59.610
for what the mean of the z's is for that hidden layer.

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:02.890
And similarly, you use an exponentially weighted average

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:07.050
to keep track of these values of sigma squared

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:10.330
that you see on the first mini-batch in that layer,

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:13.850
sigma squared that you see on the second mini-batch and so on.

00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:17.730
So you keep a running average of the mu

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:19.810
and the sigma squared that you're seeing

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:22.610
for each layer as you train the neural network

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:24.610
across different mini-banges.

00:03:24.610 --> 00:03:26.850
Then finally, at test time,

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:30.610
what you do is in place of this equation,

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:34.690
you would just compute z norm using whatever value

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:39.530
you z you have and using your exponentially weighted average

00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:41.490
of the mu and sigma squared,

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:43.810
whatever was the latest value you have

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:45.410
to do the scaling here.

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:51.410
And then you would compute z tilde on your one test example

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:54.690
using that z norm that we just computed on the left

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:58.610
and using the beta and gamma parameters

00:03:58.610 --> 00:04:02.690
that you will- you have learned during your neural network training process.

00:04:02.690 --> 00:04:06.570
So the takeaway from this is that during training time,

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:10.170
mu and sigma squared are computed on an entire mini-batch of,

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:14.690
you know, say 64, 128 or some number of examples.

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:18.210
But at test time, you might need to process a single example at a time.

00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:23.130
So the way to do that is to estimate mu and sigma squared from your training set.

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:25.290
And there are many ways to do that.

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:28.010
You could in theory run your whole training set

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:30.890
through your final network to get mu and sigma squared.

00:04:30.890 --> 00:04:32.970
But in practice, what people usually do is

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:36.730
implement an exponentially weighted average where you just keep track

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:40.090
of the mu and sigma squared values you're seeing during training

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:42.090
and use an exponentially weighted average,

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:44.170
also sometimes called a running average,

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:47.170
to just get a rough estimate of mu and sigma squared.

00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:51.370
And then you use those values of mu and sigma squared at test time

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:55.890
to do the scaling you need of the hidden unit values z.

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:58.970
In practice, this process is pretty robust

00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:03.410
to the exact way you use to estimate mu and sigma squared.

00:05:03.410 --> 00:05:07.250
So I wouldn't worry too much about exactly how you do this.

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:09.690
And if you're using a deep learning framework,

00:05:09.690 --> 00:05:15.490
they'll usually have some default way to estimate mu and sigma squared

00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:17.930
that should work reasonably well as well.

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:23.290
But in practice, any reasonable way to estimate the mean and variance

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:28.610
of your hidden unit values z should work fine at test.

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:30.450
So that's it for Dash Norm.

00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:34.090
And using it, I think you'll be able to train much deeper networks

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:37.570
and get your learning algorithm to run much more quickly.

00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:38.890
Before we wrap up for this week,

00:05:38.890 --> 00:05:43.090
I want to share with you some thoughts on deep learning frameworks as well.

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:45.370
Let's start to talk about that in the next video.
